This lecture series introduces the concept of Standardizing Flows, building on prior introductions to Flows and Application-Dependent Flows. We draw inspiration from the 2022 Florence workshop on Flows, where leading researchers from Lattice Field Physics presented an alternative view of Flows, emphasizing their role in linking multiple observations and interpretations. We extract and unify the core concepts, presenting a unified framework known as Standardizing Flows. This formulation capitalizes on insights from Lattice Field Physics, yet remains versatile to accommodate a range of domain-specific applications. We demonstrate the integrity of our approach through a combination of existing works and original research. We also offer a preliminary exploration of potential applications across three distinct research areas: Quantum Field Theory, Condensed Matter, and Machine Learning. This lecture series aims to inspire fresh insights in Standardizing Flows, paving the way for potential advancements in these research areas. We hope that Standardizing Flows will facilitate meaningful discussions across different research communities, and eventually drive joint publications in the future. 

