The growing presence of artificial intelligence systems has led to a significant need for their explanations. This requirement spans various stakeholders, including end-users, developers, and administrators, with each having distinct reasoning and desired explanation formats. Focusing on user-centric explanations is crucial to fostering trust and understanding among these stakeholders. This paper delves into the user-centric aspects of explanations by addressing three key questions. First, what are the users of AI systems? Second, what are their desired explanation formats? Lastly, how can requirements engineering support the specification of these explanations? We provide an empirical study on xAI, a leading initiative that aims to promote explainable AI, to illustrate the application of requirements engineering practices in crafting specification criteria for xAI. These findings contribute to developing a practical guide for the EXALENT approach, which offers support for the social aspects of explainable AI. This way, requirements engineering can effectively guide the development of social explainability practices, such as shaping applicable standards and creating usable tools. Ultimately, this leads to more user-centric explainable AI systems. The proposed approach is exemplified through two real-world scenarios derived from actual xAI projects. This paper draws on existing literature in this domain. The empirical data was collected during a workshop organized by the 14th International Requirements Engineering Conference. The results of this research are preliminary and will be further validated in future work. Moreover, the used materials, including the used tools, are made available at https://dx.doi.org/10.21256/ GEG-Ei-2021. 

